A||N||6||Spektyr||Sick Fiero seeks help||||4||
Z||000000||Spektyr||08-21-2001||04:08 PM||||Okay, my poor Fiero is sick again, and this time I'm not sure what the problem is.  All I do know is that it's probably really bad.<P>I'll start at the beginning...<BR>I put in a bunch of little things, the most notable being a 160 thermostat and 176 fan switch after it nearly overheated at the drive-thru.  Not surprisingly it then began to run rich.  Not being terribly worried about the extra cash I'd be spending on gas or sparkplugs, I just left it like that and figured on getting a 180 thermo later.  Ran fine for awhile, then I noticed a slight ticking noise.  That seemed to be caused by a loose heatshield, so I ignored it.<P>Then the waterpump began to squeal and the car got parked for awhile as I gathered the time and cash for a new waterpump and underdrive pulley (I was going to be in there anyway, might as well upgrade while I'm there).  I got the waterpump switched for a rebuilt one with lifetime warranty, and installed the underdrive pulley.  Everything seemed to be okay and the power boost was nice.  The only hitch in the job was I stripped the threads on the block of the small bolt directly under the waterpump pulley.  I got it in as snugly as it would go, and made sure the bolts around it were tight and left it like that.  It doesn't leak.<P>Then I begin to develop a ticking/knocking sound over 4k rpm... Not good.  Temp is good, Oil pressure is good, I figure I'll track it down next weekend.  On the way home my oil pressure begins to drop slightly, then takes a sharp dive as I pull in at my house.  The gauge reads one mark above red at idle.  Yikes.  I shut it off and find it's a bit low on oil.  Fill it up the next day, all is good.<P>Later that day however, the ticking/knocking sound steadily begins to work it's way down the rpm band and by the time I get home my oil pressure is down and the knock is showing up at idle.  Now I'm showing oil pressure at about 1/4th of the pressure range at idle, and it sounds like someone's tapping on the motor with a hammer.  Words like 'stuck lifter', 'spun bearing', and 'knocking rod' are going through my head.<P>The sound is kinda hollow sounding, no real metalling 'ping' at all, just a distinct tapping sound.  It also does not sound with every revolution, usually it goes off several times, then skips a few revolutions, then starts again.  Above 3k rpm it's pretty steady.  Also, I began to notice a definitely loss of power on the way home.<P>So... how screwed am I?  Time to just stick it in the garage and start working on the motor upgrade I wanted to do?  (Dreaming of a 3800SC or 350 SBC someday)<P>It's just so hard to pull the plug on a family member...<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||4
Z||000001||JayUSA77||08-21-2001||04:17 PM||||hmmmmmmm, oil pump okay?????<BR>J<P>------------------<BR>86' Black Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||skitime||08-21-2001||04:19 PM||||If the bottom end is going and you want to keep cost down, then pull the engine and put it on an engine stand. turn it over and remove the oil pan and the crank only.  Have the crank turned and put new bearings and put it all back together and reinstall. You might consider a new oil pump too. The engine would probably good for another 100k.  The ticking sound is probably a manifold leak so fix that while it is out. If you have the manifolds off then port them and you car will run better than ever before.<P><BR>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by skitime (edited 08-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||starfighter007@msn.com||08-21-2001||06:27 PM||||I have a feelling you are describing is a rod knock. And thats never good.If you continue to drive it be ready to get stranded somewhere.you will need all your life lines.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Spektyr||08-21-2001||09:11 PM||||Had a few more things to add.<P>Before the problem got bad, it would occasionally seem to hesistate when accelerating, normally only 2nd or 3rd gear, and usually right after 3000rpm.<P>The Haynes manual says that one of the possible causes of knocking is 'Fuel injection system in need of adjustment'.  Any thoughts on this?<P>If it turns out that the oil pump is bad, is it possible/practical to replace it from under the car?  If the motor has to come out, it won't be going back in.<p>[This message has been edited by Spektyr (edited 08-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Kasdan||08-22-2001||08:54 AM||||Fuel pump?||||reg||1
Z||000006||thomas_l||08-22-2001||02:14 PM||||Sounds like loose main or rod bearings. 1st verify oil pressure with a regular mechanical gauge. Lots of slop in the bearings will make the pressure go away at low engine speeds. Also, how many miles (and drag strip launches) since your last oil change? When I got my 87 GT on the road it was chattering over 3500 rpm. I changed to 5w-50 Syntec and no more noise. Oil pressure is a bit low when totally warmed up (~15psi) but the light isn't coming on at idle any more. I have since autocrossed it half a dozen times and put on 1000 miles with no change. Oh yeah, with all the cold running stuff make sure that rich running hasn't gassed out your oil. That might thin things out and do bad stuff to the bearings.||||reg||1
